        "orario": "15:05",
        "timestamp": 1773929148,
        "contenuto_esteso": "To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. Energy prices soar, Iran and US trade threats after Qatar gas hit Issued on: 19/03/2026 - 15:05 Oil and gas prices soared Thursday after Iran hit the world's largest liquefied natural gas (LNG) facility in Qatar and threatened to destroy the region's energy infrastructure. Donald Trump, whose country started the war alongside Israel with their attack on Iran on February 28, warned of a furious US response if such attacks continued. International benchmark Brent surged 10 percent before falling back while European gas rose 35 percent after Iran attacked Qatar's huge Ras Laffan LNG facility in retaliation for an Israeli strike on its South Pars gas field. FRANCE 24's International Affairs Commentator Angela Diffley tells us more. REPLAY: Pentagon press conference on war in Middle East 19/03/2026 'Free France': Macron reveals name of Europe's largest warship 19/03/2026 Iran: Saudi Arabia warns of 'military action' over repeated attacks 19/03/2026 Middle East Iran women's football team return after asylum tussle 19/03/2026 Gaza's Eid celebrations highlighted by losses amid ongoing conflict 19/03/2026 EU leaders pile pressure on Hungary's Orban to lift block on Ukraine loan 19/03/2026 Trump threatens to strike world's largest gas field if Iran attacks Qatar again 19/03/2026 Who is running Iran? Top leadership decimated by US-Israeli strikes 19/03/2026 Iraq caught in crossfire of Middle East war 19/03/2026"
